@h1 Using nodes h1@

This is the page where Wit clicks. Every structure in the language — a heading, a
list, a table, a link, and any component you define yourself — is the same
shape: a node. Learn the shape once and you can read and write all of them.

@h2 One shape: open, body, close h2@

A node opens with an at-sign and a name, holds a body, and closes with the same
name and an at-sign:

@@@pre
@h1 A title h1@

@blockquote A quotation, set apart. blockquote@
pre@@@

The open is the name with an at-sign in front; the close is the name with an
at-sign _after_ it; the body is everything between. The names must match — the
thing you open is the thing you close. That is the entire mechanic.

@h2 Nesting h2@

Nodes nest inside each other. Scope comes from the matching open and close pairs,
never from indentation — you indent for your own eye, and the structure is
identical however you lay it out:

@@@pre
@section
@h2 Chapter One h2@

The road climbed for an hour before the house appeared.
section@
pre@@@

@h2 Inline or block is the renderer's decision, not yours h2@

You write every node the same way. Whether it lands as an inline element woven
into a sentence or as a block of its own is decided by what the node _is_, not by
any different syntax you use. The aside is the clearest example — the identical
node is inline in one place and a block in another:

@@@pre
The keeper, @aside a rare inline remark, aside@ kept his watch.

@aside
A standalone block note.
aside@
pre@@@

You do not choose inline versus block; you just write the node, and the renderer
places it.

@h2 Nodes with no body h2@

Some nodes are self-closing — a horizontal rule, a line break — and take no body
at all. You still write an open and a close, with nothing between:

@@@pre
@hr hr@
@br br@
pre@@@

@h2 A bare reference h2@

A node name on its own, with no body, drops a value into your prose. This is how
you use a component you defined — the name stands in for its content:

@@@pre
The year was @year, and the keeper was @keeper.
pre@@@

Here @@@code @year code@@@ and @@@code @keeper code@@@ are references to values
defined elsewhere. Defining your own is covered in
@a |href /docs/write/components/| Reusable pieces a@.

@h2 Parameters, briefly h2@

When a node needs metadata — a link's address, an image's size — it rides in a
parameter, kept separate from the body. There are a few ways to write parameters,
and they all mean the same thing:

@ul
@li _Pipes_ — @@@code @a |href https://example.org| the text a@ code@@@ — one
parameter per pair of bars. li@
@li _A record argument_ — @@@code @card { title: Beta, status: draft } code@@@ —
a brace of key-and-value pairs, good for several parameters at once. li@
@li _Colon scatter_ — @@@code @card title:Beta status:draft card@ code@@@ — terse,
for a short one-line call that still has a body. li@
ul@

The full matrix of parameter forms — when to prefer each — lives in the Guides
section on parameters. For everyday writing, the content-in-body,
metadata-in-parameter rule is all you need.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A mismatched close._ Closing a node with a different name — opening a level-
two heading and closing it as level three — raises a mismatched-close error.
The names must match exactly. li@
@li _An unclosed node._ Opening a node and never closing it raises an unclosed-
node error. Every open needs its close. li@
@li _A bare reference to a name you never defined._ A node name that resolves to
nothing raises an unresolved-reference error. Define it first, or check the
spelling. li@
ul@

